77
00:17:53.160 --> 00:18:02.530
Maria Silva: were not producing slow blocks. They were at worse taking 2.5 seconds to execute. And we verified these by

78
00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:21.259
Maria Silva: looking at the actual payloads during the DevNet, but also, in the meantime, we also deployed that same attack in Benchmark Core. We are benchmarking it, and the worst client is also taking, at most, 2.5 seconds on that attack. So that's… all that…

79
00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:25.390
Maria Silva: Set of attacks that Bovell was testing. They are safe.

80
00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:33.099
Maria Silva: Now, there was blocks that were slower, and that happened before the attack that Pavel

81
00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:48.340
Maria Silva: Eitan, it was something that Jochem was, deploying. And essentially, it was also a JUMP test analysis, attack vector. And the main issue was that clients were going out of memory.

82
00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:58.089
Maria Silva: Once we looked into that, and it… we kind of, like, realized that the issue was actually because we were both running these

83
00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:10.939
Maria Silva: heavy, memory-intensive thing at the same time as we had the debug traces, on, which is not something we would expect to happen in a normal validator.

84
00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:24.219
Maria Silva: And so, we also consider, based on the results that we were already seeing also from benchmarks, that that attack is also safe. It's just, it was, compounding of

85
00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:34.029
Maria Silva: having debug traces on at the same time as we were pounding the node with this heavy attack. But without the debug traces, it would be fine.

86
00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:46.200
Maria Silva: So, yeah, so our conclusion right now, based both on benchmarks and what we've observed on DevNet, is that 200 millions is safe, at least from, yeah, from an execution time perspective, it is safe.

