127
00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:19.250
Julian Ma: Yep.

128
00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:35.959
Julian Ma: Yeah, so I wanted to share some learnings from trying to get adoption for SCR and propose a change, namely to create a new FAST block tag. So today, FCR, or the FAST Confirmation Rule, or the FAST Confirmed Blocks, are served behind the SAFE block tag, with a flag, usually.

129
00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:39.929
Julian Ma: And the proposal is to create a new FastBlock tag for FastConfirmBlocks.

130
00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:55.290
Julian Ma: The reason is that, we're seeing backwards compatibility issues. So previously, it was thought that justification, which is served behind SAFE today, wasn't used much, and that FCR has a very, comparable set of assumptions, and so

131
00:24:55.290 --> 00:25:08.380
Julian Ma: just users that would use justification would be able to move over to FCR, and there wouldn't be many users in general. We're seeing now that while people aren't necessarily arguing against that.

132
00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:16.190
Julian Ma: It is hindering adoption that RPCs want to be backwards compatible for legal reasons, for many other reasons.

133
00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:25.329
Julian Ma: And that's why they want to continue serving JustifyBlocks to their customers as well, because apparently there is a cohort of customers that relies on justification.

134
00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:42.650
Julian Ma: And these backwards compatibility concerns are really hindering adoption, which otherwise is going quite well. So Chainlink just announced that it went live with FCR today. Other bridges and L2s want to use it as well. So really, the blocker is RPC support here, and RPCs in turn are blocked by backwards compatibility.

135
00:25:43.190 --> 00:26:00.970
Julian Ma: And so the ask is to create a new FAST block tag. Mikhail already made a draft implementation, which I'm linking in the chat here. So the ask would be to look into that implementation and see how complicated it would be for you guys.

136
00:26:03.710 --> 00:26:11.060
Julian Ma: To Barnabas's point, the tag could also be FCR. I'm not very opinionated on the name of the tag.

137
00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:22.770
Justin Traglia: I'd be interested to hear what clients have to say here.

138
00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:25.410
Justin Traglia: Like, just internal.

139
00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:31.620
Justin Traglia: Dustin, would you like to speak up about, your comment.

140
00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:35.610
Dustin: Sure. Yeah.

141
00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:37.040
Dustin: So.

142
00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:45.719
Dustin: Justification could always go forward, backwards, sideways, and any which way it was, as it were, not final.

143
00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:52.599
Dustin: It was sometimes sort of propagandized as being, I was pretty sure that's going to be like this, but

144
00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:54.469
Dustin: Okay, not backwards.

145
00:26:56.440 --> 00:27:01.949
Dustin: But… That was… It was both slow.

146
00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:07.850
Dustin: Although not as slow as finalization, and also not actually final.

147
00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:21.569
Dustin: It was a middle ground which didn't make sense to begin with, and it also was changed… actually, if one looks at the history on the CL specs, maybe this is more from a spec-based argument rather than just my, you know, subjective view.

148
00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:25.410
Dustin: It actually the definition changed

149
00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:34.299
Dustin: of exactly what CL sent in forktories updated at partway through the history of the CL specs to

150
00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:37.569
Dustin: In terms of what Justified was.

151
00:27:38.110 --> 00:27:43.410
Dustin: or what SAFE was. And SAFE became…

152
00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:55.090
Dustin: sort of… the definition has already changed, basically. So, it was not consistent to begin with, and changing it again doesn't really break some kind of long-standing compatibility point.

153
00:27:55.190 --> 00:27:56.460
Dustin: From that perspective.

154
00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:10.500
Julian Ma: Yeah, I hear your points, and this is also basically the starting point that we started out with, which is why FastConfirmBlocks were served behind the Save tag, for pretty much the same reason that you're saying.

155
00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:22.220
Julian Ma: What we're seeing now is that you can use the safe tag with some additional, additional logic to create some confirmations that are meaningfully different from fast-confirmed confirmations.

156
00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:37.449
Julian Ma: And for specifics here, I would call on Francesco, but yeah, so there is a way to use justification to be, protect against different threat model than fast confirmation rule could protect you against. But I think the more important point here is just that

157
00:28:37.450 --> 00:28:48.479
Julian Ma: empirically, we are seeing, apparently large financial institutions relying on the safe tag, and they have, larger governance processes of upgrading as well. So if we…

158
00:28:48.520 --> 00:29:03.499
Julian Ma: if we don't want to create a new FAST tag, that would just hinder adoption. And so I think that's more of an empirical point and less of a first principles point, but from a first principles point as well, you could use justification in a way to be safer than the FAST information rule can be.

159
00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:24.449
Justin Traglia: Just to mention, Potuz had a good question in the chat, which was answered. He asked if RPC providers are claiming that they need to serve both, which Francesco said yes.

160
00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:27.139
Justin Traglia: That makes sense.

